        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-342b3cad3e79af40\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Constance’s words primarily present as true that she loves Lorck, even though the narration casts doubt on their sincerity and depicts them as deliberate self-persuasion. They do not independently undertake a commitment, and the repetition and ardent physical delivery do not establish expressive/reflexive force: the words follow a conscious effort to suppress disturbing feelings and inhabit a chosen conception of marital happiness.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal may be self-deceptive or strategically reassuring rather than psychologically truthful, but truth-conditional force concerns what it presents as true. Its relationally sustaining effect supplies little evidence of an undertaking distinct from the avowal itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Constance directly tells her husband, Lorck, “Jeg elsker dig,” immediately repeating the predicate as “elsker, elsker dig.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lorck explicitly asks Constance to say that she loves him. She initially experiences an urge to escape his embrace and recalls her pleasure at Mejer’s proximity; she then deliberately surrounds herself with memories of happiness with Lorck as a defensive shield. Her avowal consequently functions as a solicited declaration and an effort to sustain or manufacture her preferred emotional reality. The unusually ardent kisses, caresses, and repetition intensify that declaration but do not make it a reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck requests: “For du elsker mig, sig, at du elsker mig” (“For you love me; say that you love me”).\",\"supports\":\"The classified utterance is a solicited avowal presented as confirming the truth of Lorck’s proposition.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before speaking, Constance feels an urge to shake off his arms and flee, and remembers the pleasure she felt when Mejer put an arm on her chair.\",\"supports\":\"The narration complicates the sincerity and psychological truth of her declaration without changing its truth-conditional mode.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She deliberately enters the “circle of thoughts of love and happiness,” wraps memories around herself “like a shield,” and wills herself to be happy before speaking.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is prepared through conscious self-persuasion rather than depicted as an involuntary or reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elsker dig” — sa hun, “elsker, elsker dig,” followed by kisses and caresses more ardent than ever.\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense love avowal strongly supports T; repetition and ardent delivery indicate intensity but do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its solicitation, doubtful sincerity, self-deceptive framing, repetition, and heightened physical delivery are adequately treated as contextual features rather than additional core forces.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
